<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="geranylgeranyl pyrophosphate biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5120" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: GERANYLGERANYL-PP &quot;All-&lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-geranylgeranyl diphosphate&quot;| (also known as geranylgeranyl pyrophosphate, or GGPP) is a crucial compound involved in the biosynthesis of a variety of terpenes and terpenoids, including central compounds such as ubiquinones and menaquinones. In plants, |FRAME: GERANYLGERANYL-PP| is an intermediate in the biosynthesis of essentail compounds such as chlorophylls, carotenoids, tocopherols, plastoquinones, and gibberellins |CITS:[12427975]|, as well as a variety of secondary metabolites. In addition, GGPP is also used in posttranslational modifications of proteins (geranylgeranylation) which is important for membrane adhesion as well as the function of some proteins |CITS:[11500541][16183844]|. GGPP is synthesized from |FRAME: FARNESYL-PP| either by dedicated enzymes, such as the |FRAME: YPL069C-MONOMER| of |FRAME: TAX-4932|, or by the action of polyprenyl transferases such as the |FRAME: MONOMER-13766| from |FRAME: TAX-1061| or |FRAME: CPLX0-7426| from |FRAME: TAX-83333|. It should be noted that the building blocks for |FRAME: FARNESYL-PP| are synthesized in different organisms by different pathways. While most eukaryotes and some bacteria use the |FRAME: PWY-922|, most bacteria, as well as green algae utilize the |FRAME: NONMEVIPP-PWY| (as described in the two superpathways |FRAME: PWY-5910| and |FRAME: PWY-5121|). In plants, GGPP is synthesized using both routes - in the cytosol using the |FRAME: PWY-922|, and in the plastids using the |FRAME: NONMEVIPP-PWY|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
    <Author name="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE" />
